name: pipes and counting
boss: PipeWrench
flavor: Nothing here stands alone. It all flows through me.
brief: A pipe wires one command's stdout into the next one's stdin. That is
brief: the whole idea, and it is why this system needs so few programs: you
brief: are not given a tool for every job, you are given parts to build one.
brief:
brief: `sort` orders, `uniq` collapses neighbours, `wc` counts. `uniq` only sees the
brief: line before it, so it can only remove duplicates that are already
brief: adjacent -- which is why `sort` comes first, every time.
---
q: {%count% the lines in @log@|how many lines does @log@ have?|how long is @log@ in lines?}, with `wc`
a: wc -l < @log@
cmp: ref
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: l for lines, same flag whether you pipe it in or pass a filename
---
q: {%count% the words in @txt@|how many words are in @txt@?|word count of @txt@}, with `wc`
a: wc -w < @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: wc
teach: wc -w
hint: words are whitespace-separated, not lines
---
q: {%count% the bytes in @txt@|how many bytes is @txt@?|the size of @txt@ in bytes}, with `wc`
a: wc -c < @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: wc
teach: wc -c
hint: c counts bytes -- pair with -m for characters on multibyte text
---
q: {%count% how many entries are in the current directory|how many things are in this directory?|number of files and folders here}, with `wc`
a: ls | wc -l
out: [0-9]
cmp: ref
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: pipe a listing into wc -l to turn it into a count
---
q: {`sort` the lines of @txt@ alphabetically|put @txt@ in alphabetical order|order @txt@ a to z}
a: sort @txt@
out: ^ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort
hint: no flags -- sort defaults to alphabetical, ascending
---
q: {`sort` @txt@ in reverse alphabetical order|order @txt@ z to a with `sort`|`sort` @txt@ backwards}
a: sort -r @txt@
out: ^verify the root hash
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -r
hint: r flips the order
---
q: %show% @txt@ deduped, using `sort` alone
a: sort -u @txt@
out: ask why the fan
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -u
hint: u does the sort and the dedup in a single pass
---
q: %show% @txt@ deduped, using `sort` piped into `uniq`
a: sort @txt@ | uniq
out: ask why the fan
ref: uniq
teach: uniq
hint: uniq only collapses ADJACENT duplicates, so sort has to go first
---
q: {%show% each line of @txt@ with its count|how often does each line of @txt@ occur?|tally the repeats in @txt@}, with `uniq`
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -c
out: 2 rotate the logs
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -c
hint: c prefixes each line with how many times it appeared
---
q: %show% only the lines of @txt@ that appear more than once, with `uniq`
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -d
out: rotate the logs
cmp: ref
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -d
hint: d shows only lines that had a duplicate
---
q: %show% only the lines of @txt@ that appear exactly once, with `uniq`
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -u
out: ask why the fan
cmp: ref
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -u
hint: u here shows only lines that had no duplicate
---
q: {@csv@ is name,id,status,count -- `sort` it by status|@csv@ has columns name,id,status,count -- `sort` by status|@csv@'s columns are name,id,status,count -- `sort` by status}
a: sort -t, -k3 @csv@
out: down
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -t
hint: -t sets the separator, -k picks the field
---
q: {@csv@ is name,id,status,count -- `sort` it, highest count first|@csv@ has columns name,id,status,count -- `sort`, highest first|@csv@ is name,id,status,count -- biggest count at the top, with `sort`}
a: sort -t, -k4 -rn @csv@
a: sort -t, -k4rn @csv@
out: ^gateway,
ref: sort
teach: sort -k
hint: n makes the comparison numeric instead of text; r reverses it
---
q: {`sort` @txt@ ignoring the difference between upper and lower case|`sort` @txt@ treating uppercase and lowercase as the same|`sort` @txt@ case-insensitively}
a: sort -f @txt@
out: ask why the fan
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -f
hint: f folds case, treating upper and lower as equal
---
q: {check with `sort` whether `versions.txt` is sorted, without printing|verify with `sort` that `versions.txt` is in order, printing nothing|confirm with `sort` that `versions.txt` is sorted, silently}
a: sort -c versions.txt
status: 1
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -c
hint: it says nothing when sorted, and names the first bad line when not
---
q: {`sort` `sizes.txt`'s sizes like 2K and 1M, print only the smallest|`sort` `sizes.txt` so 2K and 1M compare right, print just the smallest|`sort` `sizes.txt` by sizes like 2K and 1M, print only the first}
a: sort -h sizes.txt | head -1
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -h
hint: h understands suffixes like K and M when comparing, so 512 sorts before 64k
---
q: {`sort` @txt@ ignoring any stray control characters|`sort` @txt@ as if the stray control characters weren't there|`sort` @txt@ so odd control characters don't throw off the order}
a: sort -i @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -i
hint: -i drops the bytes with no glyph before comparing -- case-blind sorting is -f's job
---
setup: redirecting a command's output back onto the file it's reading from empties the file before anything gets read
q: {`sort` @txt@ writing the result into `sorted.txt`|`sort` @txt@ and write the sorted result into `sorted.txt`|`sort` @txt@, saving the output into `sorted.txt` rather than printing it}
a: sort -o sorted.txt @txt@
state: exists:sorted.txt
ref: sort
teach: sort -o
hint: -o names the output file -- sort reads it all into memory before writing
---
q: {`sort` `versions.txt` so 1.10 follows 1.9, print only the last|`sort` `versions.txt`'s version numbers, print just the highest|put `versions.txt` in version order and show the newest}, with `sort`
a: sort -V versions.txt | tail -1
cmp: ref
ref: sort
teach: sort -V
hint: V understands version numbers, so 1.9 sorts before 1.10, and 2.0 sorts last of all
---
q: {collapse duplicate lines of @txt@ ignoring case|collapse duplicates in @txt@, treating case as irrelevant|remove case-insensitive duplicate lines from @txt@}, with `uniq`
a: sort -f @txt@ | uniq -i
cmp: ref
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -i
hint: sort -f groups case-insensitively, uniq -i then collapses it
